        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for such a detailed suggestion!\r\n\r\nBasically, you laid out everything just right:\r\n- `<C-z>` remapping is intentional. The only two letters relatable to spelling are `s` (as from **s**pelling) and `z` (many spelling Normal mode commands start with `z`). As `<C-s>` is more associated with \"save\", `<C-z>` is used.\r\n- I don't think that `<C-z>` built-in **mapping** is *that* useful. After seeing that comment and getting to know about `fg` to go back, I think so less, but not enough to change current 'mini.basic's behavior. \r\n- Indeed if any mapping overrides the built-in default one, users can delete it after `require('mini.basics').setup()`. This is [mentioned in option description](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/doc/mini-basics.txt#L200) which seems to be the most direct place to put it. There are also other approaches:\r\n    - Put `vim.keymap.set('n', '<C-z>', '<Cmd>stop<CR>')` anywhere in user config. This is more explicit than deleting mapping.\r\n    - Use `:stop` explicitly instead of `<C-z>`.\r\n\r\nAll in all, I don't think this will be changed. That said, I really appreciate your time to create this issue! Hope you'll enjoy 'mini.nvim' otherwise.",
